1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:07,977
Imagine you are responsible for a neighborhood
where hard rain can overwhelm the drains. You

2
00:00:07,977 --> 00:00:13,909
have 220 improvement plans and no budget to
build them all. So you draw a

3
00:00:13,909 --> 00:00:20,411
map: cost along the bottom, and how much less
water reaches the outlet along the

4
00:00:20,411 --> 00:00:26,994
side. One plan looks like a bargain. It costs
about 55 thousand dollars and sends

5
00:00:26,994 --> 00:00:33,577
a little less water out. But the picture has
hidden something. In the long storm,

6
00:00:33,577 --> 00:00:39,834
the highest flow rate rises from 3.14 to 3.21
cubic feet per second. A second

7
00:00:39,834 --> 00:00:46,580
storm rises too. And the plan still misses our 6
percent reduction target. The plot

8
00:00:46,580 --> 00:00:53,000
did not lie. It answered a smaller question. A
good decision must ask the rest.

9
00:00:55,500 --> 00:01:01,806
This is what GI Scenario Studio is for:
comparing ways to change how much runoff

10
00:01:01,806 --> 00:01:08,901
reaches a drainage system, and how quickly it
arrives. Engineers call these features green

11
00:01:08,901 --> 00:01:16,311
infrastructure—rain gardens, porous pavement,
planted basins, and rain barrels. If you already

12
00:01:16,311 --> 00:01:22,459
have a drainage model, you can open it here. For
this walkthrough, we will use

13
00:01:22,459 --> 00:01:29,081
Cedar Trace, a tiny fictional neighborhood built
for teaching. It is not a real town

14
00:01:29,081 --> 00:01:35,939
or a design recommendation. It gives us
something safer: a complete example we can take

15
00:01:35,939 --> 00:01:42,640
apart. The demo stays on this computer; cloud
sharing is off. Our question is simple:

16
00:01:42,640 --> 00:01:48,000
which plan, if any, deserves the cost of a real
field investigation?

17
00:01:50,500 --> 00:01:56,689
Before we use any software, follow the water.
Rain lands on an area that drains

18
00:01:56,689 --> 00:02:03,582
to the same place—engineers call that area a
catchment. Some water soaks into soil. Some

19
00:02:03,582 --> 00:02:09,614
waits in a shallow basin or rain garden. The
rest runs across the surface and

20
00:02:09,614 --> 00:02:16,978
into pipes. Eventually it reaches an outfall,
simply the point where water leaves the drainage

21
00:02:16,978 --> 00:02:23,167
model. The flow there rises and falls during a
storm. The total area under this

22
00:02:23,167 --> 00:02:29,512
curve is event volume: all the water that
passed. The highest point is peak flow:

23
00:02:29,512 --> 00:02:36,406
the highest modeled flow rate during the storm.
A project can lower one without lowering

24
00:02:36,406 --> 00:02:42,516
the other. A different rain pattern can change
both. That is why one plan must

25
00:02:42,516 --> 00:02:48,000
face more than one storm. These drawings are
conceptual, not measured.

26
00:02:50,500 --> 00:02:56,898
Now we can introduce SWMM, the EPA's Storm Water
Management Model. Think of it as

27
00:02:56,898 --> 00:03:03,690
a calculator for a simplified drainage world.
The engineer supplies a map of the land,

28
00:03:03,690 --> 00:03:11,036
junctions, pipes, and outlet; a rain pattern;
and the green-infrastructure plan to test. SWMM

29
00:03:11,036 --> 00:03:17,907
follows those declared rules and produces
modeled flows and water levels. The app reads

30
00:03:17,907 --> 00:03:25,332
the output, checks that the run completed and
its numerical balance stayed within the declared

31
00:03:25,332 --> 00:03:32,282
limit, and gives each file a digital fingerprint
for later. But a careful calculation is

32
00:03:32,282 --> 00:03:38,127
still only as good as the world described to it.
SWMM cannot see a missing

33
00:03:38,127 --> 00:03:44,604
pipe, test the soil, or tell us whether the
chosen storm represents reality. Exact

34
00:03:44,604 --> 00:03:48,000
calculation is not the same as field truth.

35
00:03:50,500 --> 00:03:58,559
Before looking at answers, we write down what
good enough means. This matters: if we

36
00:03:58,559 --> 00:04:07,098
invent the rules after seeing a favorite, the
analysis becomes a story we tell ourselves.

37
00:04:07,098 --> 00:04:16,117
Our fictional engineer sets four rules. First,
across four practice storms, at least 6 percent

38
00:04:16,117 --> 00:04:24,752
less water must reach the model's outlet than in
the unchanged model. That unchanged model

39
00:04:24,752 --> 00:04:33,003
is our baseline. Second, the 30-year screening
cost must stay at or below 200 thousand

40
00:04:33,003 --> 00:04:40,582
dollars. Third, no storm is allowed to create a
higher peak flow at the outlet.

41
00:04:40,582 --> 00:04:49,313
A good total cannot excuse one sharper surge.
Fourth, every simulation used in the decision

42
00:04:49,313 --> 00:04:57,756
must pass basic numerical checks. The storms and
costs are invented for this lesson. And

43
00:04:57,756 --> 00:05:05,911
verified replay means the saved files still
match the recorded runs. It does not mean

44
00:05:05,911 --> 00:05:14,066
the model matches a real neighborhood. With the
question and rules fixed, we can look

45
00:05:14,066 --> 00:05:18,000
at evidence without moving the goalposts.

46
00:05:20,500 --> 00:05:27,599
Picture four lights wired together. Under this
teaching brief, a plan advances only when all

47
00:05:27,599 --> 00:05:34,157
four are green: enough volume reduction,
acceptable cost, no peak increase, and clean

48
00:05:34,157 --> 00:05:41,179
runs. ALT-207 turns on all four: more than 6
percent reduction, under 200 thousand dollars,

49
00:05:41,179 --> 00:05:47,429
no higher peak, and four clean runs. ALT-017 is
cheaper, but two lights stay red.

50
00:05:47,429 --> 00:05:53,370
It misses the volume target and raises two storm
peaks. We do not average red

51
00:05:53,370 --> 00:05:58,000
lights into green ones. One required failure
stops the plan.

52
00:06:00,500 --> 00:06:10,138
Before comparing plans, the app checks the
unchanged model and the saved study. It

53
00:06:10,138 --> 00:06:21,186
identifies the exact software version, confirms
that every required file is present, opens the

54
00:06:21,186 --> 00:06:30,236
model, and confirms that the four stored
starting runs completed. The largest

55
00:06:30,236 --> 00:06:41,637
water-accounting mismatch is less than one tenth
of one percent, comfortably inside this lesson's

56
00:06:41,637 --> 00:06:51,275
warning limit. So the saved calculation
completed cleanly. One caution remains. No

57
00:06:51,275 --> 00:07:02,206
observed storm and no trusted prior result were
supplied for comparison. The screen therefore

58
00:07:02,206 --> 00:07:11,139
says model fitness: not assessed. That label is
not a failure, and it is not

59
00:07:11,139 --> 00:07:20,894
permission. It tells us that the saved
calculation is inspectable while the model's

60
00:07:20,894 --> 00:07:31,942
connection to a real place remains unknown. The
acknowledgment simply records that we saw this

61
00:07:31,942 --> 00:07:33,000
boundary.

62
00:07:35,500 --> 00:07:41,621
A navigation app can follow its map perfectly
even when a real road has closed.

63
00:07:41,621 --> 00:07:48,517
Drainage models have the same two questions. Did
the software run cleanly and account for

64
00:07:48,517 --> 00:07:55,335
the water? For Cedar Trace, yes. Does this
simplified model describe a real neighborhood

65
00:07:55,335 --> 00:08:02,075
well enough for this decision? We do not know.
No observed events or approved reference

66
00:08:02,075 --> 00:08:09,126
result were supplied. Clean execution earns
confidence in the calculation. It does not earn

67
00:08:09,126 --> 00:08:13,000
confidence in the world the calculation
describes.

68
00:08:15,500 --> 00:08:25,220
Rainfall is not just a depth; timing matters. An
inch falling gradually can interact with

69
00:08:25,220 --> 00:08:34,612
storage very differently from two inches
arriving mostly at the beginning. Cedar Trace

70
00:08:34,612 --> 00:08:43,676
uses four artificial rain patterns so we can see
that difference. One inch over one

71
00:08:43,676 --> 00:08:52,413
hour. Two inches over one hour, weighted toward
the front. Two and a half inches

72
00:08:52,413 --> 00:09:01,914
spread over four hours. And a four-inch,
two-hour stress test. These are not historical

73
00:09:01,914 --> 00:09:11,852
storms or official design events. They are
practice questions. Everything else in the model

74
00:09:11,852 --> 00:09:21,135
stays the same while the rain changes, so the
comparison is fair inside this example.

75
00:09:21,135 --> 00:09:30,963
One surprising lesson will come from the long,
four-hour event: it creates ALT-017's worst

76
00:09:30,963 --> 00:09:40,137
increase at the outlet even though another storm
has a much larger overall peak. The

77
00:09:40,137 --> 00:09:48,000
biggest storm is not automatically the one that
controls every decision.

78
00:09:50,500 --> 00:09:56,336
Now take one plan and run it through all four
rain patterns. We get four

79
00:09:56,336 --> 00:10:03,307
answers, not one. For total volume, adding the
four storm totals is useful because our

80
00:10:03,307 --> 00:10:10,197
teaching rule asks about their combined
reduction. For peak flow, adding or averaging

81
00:10:10,197 --> 00:10:16,763
would destroy the question we care about: did
any single storm get worse? So each

82
00:10:16,763 --> 00:10:24,301
peak keeps its own before-and-after comparison.
ALT-017 lowers two peaks, but raises two. The

83
00:10:24,301 --> 00:10:30,786
long storm is worst, up 0.07 cubic feet per
second. If we squash everything into

84
00:10:30,786 --> 00:10:37,433
one score, that red warning can disappear. Every
summary is a choice about what to

85
00:10:37,433 --> 00:10:38,000
forget.

86
00:10:40,500 --> 00:10:48,543
Now we decide what the software is allowed to
change. Not pipe sizes. Not soil

87
00:10:48,543 --> 00:10:57,618
properties. Not the design of a rain garden.
Only four whole-number counts of additional

88
00:10:57,618 --> 00:11:06,796
units at four teaching locations: planted
bioretention, areas of permeable pavement, rain

89
00:11:06,796 --> 00:11:15,355
gardens, and groups of rain barrels. Each
location has a maximum count and a stated

90
00:11:15,355 --> 00:11:24,945
drainage destination. The map is a diagram, not
surveyed geometry. The engineering details of

91
00:11:24,945 --> 00:11:34,123
each practice stay fixed. The search can ask,
for example, what happens after adding zero

92
00:11:34,123 --> 00:11:43,713
bioretention units, three pavement units, two
rain-garden units, and six barrel groups to the

93
00:11:43,713 --> 00:11:52,273
unchanged model? It cannot invent a new soil mix
or pretend a utility conflict does

94
00:11:52,273 --> 00:12:02,069
not exist. Automation is useful when it explores
choices an engineer has deliberately admitted.

95
00:12:02,069 --> 00:12:11,350
Here, demo approved means allowed into the
lesson—not feasible, constructible, or approved

96
00:12:11,350 --> 00:12:13,000
for a real site.

97
00:12:15,500 --> 00:12:23,910
Those four additional-unit counts work like four
dials. The first has five settings, the second

98
00:12:23,910 --> 00:12:31,080
four, the third five, and the fourth seven.
Multiply them and we get 700 possible

99
00:12:31,080 --> 00:12:39,136
combinations, including the unchanged baseline.
Testing every combination under four storms

100
00:12:39,136 --> 00:12:47,103
would require 2,800 SWMM runs. That complete
study exists as a benchmark. The guided story

101
00:12:47,103 --> 00:12:55,512
is smaller: 220 chosen combinations, four storms
each, plus four baseline runs—884 runs in all.

102
00:12:55,512 --> 00:13:03,125
It is not every possibility, and it is not a
demonstration that machine learning found

103
00:13:03,125 --> 00:13:10,738
a better answer. Imagine the untested
combinations as face-down cards. A search method

104
00:13:10,738 --> 00:13:17,289
may choose which card to turn over next. That
choice is a question, not an

105
00:13:17,289 --> 00:13:25,167
answer. Only a direct SWMM run can reveal the
modeled hydraulics. Search decides where to

106
00:13:25,167 --> 00:13:28,000
look. SWMM supplies what we see.

107
00:13:30,500 --> 00:13:39,618
The four plain-language rules now become checks
the computer can apply consistently. Water

108
00:13:39,618 --> 00:13:48,330
volume is measured at the same outlet and
compared with the same unchanged model. Peak

109
00:13:48,330 --> 00:13:58,056
flow is checked separately for every storm. The
teaching cost rolls installation, future upkeep,

110
00:13:58,056 --> 00:14:06,565
and replacement over 30 years into one
today-dollar screening estimate. It keeps the

111
00:14:06,565 --> 00:14:15,278
lesson consistent; it is not a contractor's
price or a local budget. Every option also

112
00:14:15,278 --> 00:14:23,889
needs four completed SWMM runs and clean
numerical checks. If this tiny model reports

113
00:14:23,889 --> 00:14:32,399
zero for a flooding measure, that remains a
modeled result—not a promise that a real

114
00:14:32,399 --> 00:14:39,794
street stays dry. The point of this screen is
not to add more numbers. It

115
00:14:39,794 --> 00:14:48,000
is to stop the meaning of success from changing
after an attractive plan appears.

116
00:14:50,500 --> 00:14:56,592
Here is where 884 comes from. Put the four
storms across the top. The unchanged

117
00:14:56,592 --> 00:15:02,144
model fills the first four squares. Then each of
220 plans gets a row of

118
00:15:02,144 --> 00:15:08,544
four more squares—one real SWMM run per storm.
Four plus 220 times four equals 884.

119
00:15:08,544 --> 00:15:15,407
Each square keeps the result, the numerical
check, and digital fingerprints of its files.

120
00:15:15,407 --> 00:15:22,347
The project also keeps complete input, report,
and output files for the four baseline runs

121
00:15:22,347 --> 00:15:29,441
and for four teaching plans under four storms:
20 full evidence sets. Opening those finished

122
00:15:29,441 --> 00:15:35,455
records is a replay. Running all 884 again would
be a new experiment. A replay

123
00:15:35,455 --> 00:15:38,000
is a receipt, not a progress bar.

124
00:15:40,500 --> 00:15:49,779
This screen is showing receipts, not pretending
to run a simulation. The 884 jobs were

125
00:15:49,779 --> 00:15:58,842
completed earlier and stored in a manifest—a
list of what ran and which files belong

126
00:15:58,842 --> 00:16:07,473
to each result. It names the fixed SWMM version,
the 220 plans, the four storms,

127
00:16:07,473 --> 00:16:16,212
and the numerical check for every job. The plans
were ordered in a repeatable way

128
00:16:16,212 --> 00:16:25,599
chosen in advance; machine learning did not
select this guided set. A local rerun would

129
00:16:25,599 --> 00:16:34,877
be different: the app would launch SWMM again,
show real progress, and compare the new

130
00:16:34,877 --> 00:16:43,832
answers with the stored ones. That control
appears only when the expected engine is

131
00:16:43,832 --> 00:16:52,787
actually available. It is not available in this
recording, so the app does not fake

132
00:16:52,787 --> 00:17:02,066
it. Replay lets us inspect what happened. Rerun
lets us test whether it happens again.

133
00:17:02,066 --> 00:17:08,000
Neither should be confused with work currently
running.

134
00:17:10,500 --> 00:17:20,575
Now the plans become characters rather than
dots. First is the bargain-looking plan,

135
00:17:20,575 --> 00:17:31,130
ALT-017: about 55 thousand dollars and just over
4 percent less total water. That misses

136
00:17:31,130 --> 00:17:40,966
our target, and its long-storm peak rises from
3.14 to 3.21 cubic feet per second.

137
00:17:40,966 --> 00:17:50,801
Cheap is not the same as acceptable. Next is
ALT-207: about 6.4 percent less water

138
00:17:50,801 --> 00:18:00,397
for 190 thousand dollars. None of its four peaks
rises, and every run passes its

139
00:18:00,397 --> 00:18:10,112
checks. It clears this teaching screen, so it is
a candidate to carry forward for

140
00:18:10,112 --> 00:18:20,427
site and feasibility review—a question to
investigate, not an approval. ALT-109 offers

141
00:18:20,427 --> 00:18:29,543
about 7 percent reduction, but its
222-thousand-dollar cost crosses the cap.

142
00:18:29,543 --> 00:18:39,978
ALT-014 gives the strongest volume reduction
seen among all 220 guided plans, about 8.8

143
00:18:39,978 --> 00:18:51,253
percent, but costs roughly 362 thousand dollars.
More hydraulic benefit can still be the wrong

144
00:18:51,253 --> 00:19:01,328
fit for the brief. The detailed values remain on
screen, while the app evaluates the

145
00:19:01,328 --> 00:19:12,123
unrounded stored numbers. Among these four
examples, one clears every rule. We have earned

146
00:19:12,123 --> 00:19:18,000
a smaller set of questions, not a design
verdict.

147
00:19:20,500 --> 00:19:26,062
Why did ALT-017 look so good at the beginning?
Because a flat plot is a

148
00:19:26,062 --> 00:19:33,269
shadow. Here, better means farther left for
lower cost and higher for more volume reduction.

149
00:19:33,269 --> 00:19:38,909
Along the visible edge, no other tested plan is
at least as cheap and at

150
00:19:38,909 --> 00:19:45,960
least as effective while improving one of those
measures. Engineers call a point like that

151
00:19:45,960 --> 00:19:52,697
nondominated, and the edge a Pareto frontier.
The names sound like verdicts. They only

152
00:19:52,697 --> 00:20:00,217
describe this two-number picture. Bring back the
6 percent target and ALT-017 falls short. Bring

153
00:20:00,217 --> 00:20:06,719
back each storm's peak and two red increases
appear. Then remember that the model's

154
00:20:06,719 --> 00:20:13,535
real-world fitness is still unknown. A Pareto
frontier is a useful map of one tradeoff.

155
00:20:13,535 --> 00:20:18,000
It is not the border between acceptable and
unacceptable.

156
00:20:20,500 --> 00:20:32,061
Now the plot has a proper job: exploration. The
teal edge shows the cost-versus-volume tradeoff

157
00:20:32,061 --> 00:20:40,823
we just named. Each gray dot has a tested
competitor that is at least as

158
00:20:40,823 --> 00:20:50,559
cheap and effective, and better on one of those
measures. The ochre dots are our

159
00:20:50,559 --> 00:21:01,755
four teaching plans with complete files ready to
inspect. Select ALT-017 beside ALT-207. The

160
00:21:01,755 --> 00:21:12,221
first is cheaper. The second clears the full
brief. Neither fact cancels the other. We

161
00:21:12,221 --> 00:21:23,174
can change the vertical measure, open the four
storm results, or read the accessible table

162
00:21:23,174 --> 00:21:34,248
instead of the chart. Each view opens another
window onto the same stored simulations; none

163
00:21:34,248 --> 00:21:43,132
creates a new run. Use the plot to decide where
to look. Use the declared

164
00:21:43,132 --> 00:21:48,000
rules to decide what passes this screen.

165
00:21:50,500 --> 00:21:57,212
Take one red number: plus 0.07 cubic feet per
second. Imagine handing it to another

166
00:21:57,212 --> 00:22:04,006
engineer. Their first question should be,
compared with what? The answer is the long

167
00:22:04,006 --> 00:22:10,961
storm: 3.14 in the unchanged model, 3.21 in
ALT-017. Then comes the receipt. This plan

168
00:22:10,961 --> 00:22:18,239
added one rain-garden unit and six rain-barrel
units. The project stores that compactly as

169
00:22:18,239 --> 00:22:24,305
zero, zero, one, six. It wrote the change into
an input file, ran the fixed

170
00:22:24,305 --> 00:22:31,098
SWMM version, kept the report and detailed
output, identified the files with digital

171
00:22:31,098 --> 00:22:38,700
fingerprints, and recorded the numerical check.
That chain is called provenance: a path from a

172
00:22:38,700 --> 00:22:44,523
claim back to what created it. It shows how the
number was made. It does

173
00:22:44,523 --> 00:22:48,000
not prove the model matches the real world.

174
00:22:50,500 --> 00:22:59,781
Inside the app, open ALT-017 and ask for the
built-in explanation. It does something

175
00:22:59,781 --> 00:23:09,503
deliberately unglamorous: it copies both
recorded peak failures from local evidence. The

176
00:23:09,503 --> 00:23:18,342
long event rises from 3.14 to 3.21 cubic feet
per second; the stress event rises

177
00:23:18,342 --> 00:23:27,622
from 13.33 to 13.34. These are not values
remembered by a language model. Just below

178
00:23:27,622 --> 00:23:38,008
them, the model-backed Evidence Guide is held
behind its release gate. The evaluation selected

179
00:23:38,008 --> 00:23:47,730
no model, so the interface makes no inference
call and substitutes no plausible-sounding

180
00:23:47,730 --> 00:23:57,232
answer. That is what fail closed means: the
optional explanation disappears, while the

181
00:23:57,232 --> 00:24:07,396
evidence stays useful. Now move to ALT-207. It
passes the four synthetic teaching rules, but

182
00:24:07,396 --> 00:24:17,119
hydrology is not ecology. These runs do not
measure water quality, groundwater recharge,

183
00:24:17,119 --> 00:24:27,725
habitat, heat, actual flooding, maintenance, or
lifecycle effects. The rail keeps what the model

184
00:24:27,725 --> 00:24:36,785
supports beside what remains unknown.
Computation can organize evidence; it cannot

185
00:24:36,785 --> 00:24:38,000
enlarge it.

186
00:24:40,500 --> 00:24:50,026
The final product is not a winning dot. It is a
handoff another engineer can

187
00:24:50,026 --> 00:25:00,930
question. The package carries the original
question, the rules, the plans compared, the

188
00:25:00,930 --> 00:25:12,211
results, the model runs behind them, and the
limitations that still matter. The app checks

189
00:25:12,211 --> 00:25:22,488
those pieces before enabling the learning
export, then records which cases include

190
00:25:22,488 --> 00:25:34,020
complete input, report, and detailed output
files. For this recording, we download a compact

191
00:25:34,020 --> 00:25:44,423
local summary. W&B can mirror the evidence for
private collaboration only after the

192
00:25:44,423 --> 00:25:56,330
destination's access policy is verified. It is
not verified here, so nothing uploads. The local

193
00:25:56,330 --> 00:26:07,610
receipt remains available. A good export does
not make the conclusion larger. It makes the

194
00:26:07,610 --> 00:26:13,000
work easier for the next person to inspect.

195
00:26:15,500 --> 00:26:22,482
ALT-207 passes the teaching screen. Now the
question moves outside the computer. Is there

196
00:26:22,482 --> 00:26:28,915
space? Where are the utilities? Will the soil
soak up water? Who maintains it? Can

197
00:26:28,915 --> 00:26:36,290
it be built? Are the storms, costs, and model
